/* Serglige Con Culainn ocus Óenét Emire — "The Wasting Sickness of Cú
   Chulainn, and the Only Jealousy of Emer". Old and Middle Irish prose and
   verse, preserved in a single manuscript: Lebor na hUidre (RIA MS 23 E 25,
   pp. 43–50, before 1106), which says it copied it from the lost Yellow Book
   of Slane. The text is a compilation of (at least) two versions: the
   first part calls the hero's wife Ethne Inguba, the later part Emer, and an
   unrelated piece (the bull-feast at Tara and Cú Chulainn's instructions to
   Lugaid, §§21–27) has been set into it.

   The source column is Ernst Windisch's edition, Irische Texte I (Leipzig,
   1880), pp. 205–227 — public domain. Transcribed from the Internet Archive
   scan; abbreviations as Windisch expands them, his section numbers kept.
   Windisch gives a German summary but no translation; A. H. Leahy's English
   (Heroic Romances of Ireland I, 1905) is the public-domain comparison.

   The English here is an original translation. The obscure "rosc" passages
   (§§17–19, 28, 40) are translated tentatively and marked so. The prose is
   complete; the longer poems (§§29, 31, 33–34, 37, 44–45), the king's
   instructions (§§25–26) and the blemish aside in §5 are abridged, and
   each abridgement is marked in its note.
   Attaches to window.SERGLIGE. */
window.SERGLIGE = window.SERGLIGE || {};
window.SERGLIGE.tale = {
  meta: {
    blurb: "<strong>Serglige Con Culainn</strong>, <em>the Wasting Sickness of Cú Chulainn</em>, is the Samhain tale of the otherworld as desire. The Ulstermen are at their great Samhain assembly on the plain of Muirthemne. Two birds linked by a chain of red gold settle on the lake and sing the host to sleep. Cú Chulainn throws at them and, for the first time in his life, misses. He sits down with his back against a pillar-stone and falls asleep. Two women come to him, one in a green cloak and one in crimson. Smiling, they beat him with horsewhips until he is nearly dead. He lies speechless in his sickbed for a year.<br><br>On the eve of the next Samhain a stranger sits on the end of his bed and sings him an invitation to Mag Mell, the Plain of Delight. There Fand, the wife Manannán son of Ler has left, loves him. Her sister Lí Ban will heal him if he fights one day for her husband Labraid. He goes, fights, and stays a month with Fand. Then comes the tryst at the strand, where his wife Emer arrives with fifty women and sharpened knives. It is the one jealousy of Emer. Fand gives him up, and her husband the sea-god comes for her. Druids give Cú Chulainn and Emer a drink of forgetting, and Manannán shakes his cloak between Cú Chulainn and Fand so that they never meet again. The scribe ends by explaining that all of it was the work of demons.<br><br>Below is the tale in ten movements, following Windisch's 49 sections: <strong>all of the prose</strong>, with the longest poems abridged (each one is marked). The Irish is his 1880 edition of Lebor na hUidre, and the English is an <strong>original translation</strong>.",
    sources: [
      { label: "Ernst Windisch, Irische Texte I (Leipzig, 1880), pp. 197–227 — edition from Lebor na hUidre, with German summary (public domain)", url: "https://archive.org/details/irischetextemitw01winduoft/page/205/mode/2up", host: "Internet Archive" },
      { label: "A. H. Leahy, Heroic Romances of Ireland I (1905) — English translation (public domain)", url: "https://archive.org/details/heroicromancesof01leah", host: "Internet Archive" },
      { label: "The Wasting Sickness of Cú Chulainn — overview", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Wasting_Sickness_of_C%C3%BA_Chulainn", host: "Wikipedia" },
    ],
  },
  roadmap: [
    { t: "Source text transcribed (Windisch 1880, LU)", done: true },
    { t: "I · The Samhain assembly", done: true },
    { t: "II · The birds on the lake", done: true },
    { t: "III · The two women at the pillar-stone", done: true },
    { t: "IV · A year in bed; a stranger at Samhain", done: true },
    { t: "V · Lí Ban; Láeg goes to Mag Mell", done: true },
    { t: "VI · The bull-feast at Tara (an interpolation)", done: true },
    { t: "VII · Emer", done: true },
    { t: "VIII · Into the otherworld; the battle", done: true },
    { t: "IX · The tryst at the strand", done: true },
    { t: "X · Manannán's cloak", done: true },
  ],
  passages: [
    {
      title: "I. The Samhain assembly",
      segments: [
        { w: "§1. Oenach dognithe la Ultu cecha bliadna, .i. tri lá ría samfuin ocus tri laa iarma ocus lathe na samna feisne. Iss ed eret no bitis Ulaid insin im-Maig Murthemni oc ferthain óenaig na samna cecha bliadna, ocus ni ra be isin bith ni dognethe in n-eret sin leú acht cluchi ocus céti ocus ánius ocus aibinnius ocus longad ocus tomailt, conid desin atát na trénse samna sechnón na hErend.",
          e: "The Ulstermen held an assembly every year: three days before Samhain, three days after it, and the day of Samhain itself. That was how long the Ulstermen spent on the plain of Muirthemne holding the Samhain assembly every year, and nothing in the world was done in that time but games and gatherings, splendour and delight, eating and feasting. And that is where the three days of Samhain come from, all over Ireland.",
          n: "The most explicit Samhain calendar in early Irish literature: <em>tri lá ría samfuin ocus tri laa iarma ocus lathe na samna feisne</em>. Seven days, centred on Samhain. The tale explains a custom, as Nera's tale did: <em>conid desin atát na trénse samna</em>, “that is why there are the three days of Samhain”. <strong>Compare Aided Muirchertaig</strong>, whose catastrophe falls in Samhain week too." },
        { w: "§2. Fechtas and tra fertha oenach la hUltu im-Maig Murthemni, ocus ba hairi no fertha leu fobith tabarta do chách a chomraime ocus a gascid do gres cecha samna. Ba bés leu dano diag inna comraime ferthain ind óenaig, .i. rind aurlabra cech fir no marbtais do thabairt inna m-bossán … Ocus is amlaid dognitis sin ocus a claidib for a sliastaib, in tan dognítís in comram. Ar imsóitis a claidib friu, in tan dognitis gúchomram. Deithbir ón, ar no labraitis demna friu ina n-armaib, conid de batir comarddi forro a n-airm.",
          e: "Once, then, the Ulstermen were holding the assembly on the plain of Muirthemne. The reason they held it was so that every man could tell his triumphs and his feats of arms, every Samhain. It was their custom at the assembly to bring the tips of the tongues of every man they had killed, in their pouches, to count their kills. And they told their triumphs with their swords across their thighs; for their swords would turn against them when they made a false boast. No wonder: demons used to speak to them out of their weapons, which is why their weapons were a safeguard against them.",
          n: "A rule of speech enforced by the otherworld: the sword on the thigh turns on a liar. <em>no labraitis demna friu ina n-armaib</em>, “demons used to speak to them from their weapons”. This is the Christian scribe's word, as in Nera, for what the tale treats as ordinary." },
        { w: "§3. Tancatar Ulaid uli dochom ind oenaig acht dias namma, .i. Conall Cernach ocus Fergus mac Roig. «Ferthair in n-óenach», ol Ulaid. «Ni firfidir», ol Cuculainn, «co ti Conall ocus Fergus», fo bith ba haite dó Fergus ocus ba comalta Conall Cernach. Asbert Sencha iarom: «Imberthar fidcella dún coléic, ocus canitir drechta, ocus agat clesamnaig.» Dognither iarom ani sin. A m-bátar and iarom, tairnid enlaith forsin loch ocaib. Ni batar i n-Ere enlaith ba cáini.",
          e: "All the Ulstermen came to the assembly except two: Conall Cernach and Fergus mac Róich. “Let the assembly be held,” said the Ulstermen. “It will not be held,” said Cú Chulainn, “until Conall and Fergus come” — for Fergus was his foster-father and Conall his foster-brother. Then Sencha said: “Let's play fidchell for now, and have poems sung, and let the jugglers perform.” So it was done. While they were there, a flock of birds settled on the lake near them. There were no lovelier birds in Ireland.",
          n: "The assembly is waiting, held back by Cú Chulainn's loyalty, and the otherworld arrives as birds on the lake." },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "II. The birds on the lake",
      segments: [
        { w: "§4. Batar imtholtanaig na mná im na heónu. Gabais cách díb immarbaig ammuin a celi im gabail na n-en. Asbert Ethne Aitencáithrech ben Conchobair: «Asagussim én cechtar mo da gúaland dind enlaith ucut.» «Assagussem uli», ol na mná, «ani sin.» «Má gabtair do neoch, is dam-sa ceta gebthar», ol Ethne Inguba ben Conculaind. «Cid dogénam?» ol na mná. «Ninsa», for Leborcham ingen Oa ocus Adairce, «rega-sa úaib do chuinchid Conculaind.»",
          e: "The women were all eager for the birds. They began boasting to each other about catching them. Ethne Aitencháithrech, Conchobar's wife, said: “I want a bird on each of my two shoulders from that flock.” “We all want that,” said the women. “If they're caught for anyone, they'll be caught for me first,” said Ethne Inguba, Cú Chulainn's wife. “What shall we do?” said the women. “Easy,” said Leborcham, daughter of Óa and Adarc. “I'll go from you to ask Cú Chulainn.”",
          n: "<em>Ethne Inguba</em>: in this first part of the tale Cú Chulainn's wife has this name. From §28 she is Emer. The tale was put together from two versions and the seam shows." },
        { w: "§5. Luid iarom co Coinculaind ocus asbert fris: «Is ail dona mnáib ind eoin ucut úait-siu.» Atetha a claideb do imbert furri. «Ni fogbat merdrecha Ulad anaill acht foraim én dóib do thabairt fornd indiu.» «Ni cóir duit ém», for Leborcham, «fúasnad friu, ar is triut ata in tres anim fil for mnáib Ulad, .i. guille.»",
          e: "She went to Cú Chulainn and said to him: “The women want those birds from you.” He reached for his sword to strike her. “The whores of Ulster can find nothing better to do than send us bird-hunting today!” “You've no right to be angry with them,” said Leborcham, “for it's because of you that the women of Ulster have the third of their blemishes: one-eyedness.”",
          n: "A strange, comic aside, abridged here from Windisch's text: every woman who loved Conall went crooked, every woman who loved Cúscraid stammered, and every woman who loved Cú Chulainn half-closed one eye in imitation of him, because when he was angry one eye sank into his head. The hero's body imprints itself on the women who desire him, the theme of the whole tale in miniature." },
        { w: "§6. «Indel dún in carpat, a Laích!» ol Cuculaind. Indlis Lóeg iarom in carpat, ocus téit Cuculaind sin carpat ocus ataig taithbéim dia claidiub dóib, co ruildetar a m-bossa ocus a n-eti dind usciu. Nos gaibet uli iarom ocus dos bertatar leo, ocus fodailset dona mnáib, conná ra bi ben nad rissed da én diib acht Ethne Inguba a hóenur. Tánic de iarom co a mnái fessin. «Is olc do menma», ol Cuculaind fria. «Ni olc», ol Ethne, «úair is uaim fodailter doib.» … «Ná bad olc do menma trá», ol Cuculaind. «Dia tisat eoin Mag Murthemni no Boind, in da en ba haildem dib, dut icfat.»",
          e: "“Harness the chariot for us, Láeg!” said Cú Chulainn. Láeg harnessed it, and Cú Chulainn went out in the chariot and struck at the birds with the flat of his sword, so that their feet and wings stuck to the water. Then they all caught them and brought them back and shared them among the women, until there was no woman without two birds except Ethne Inguba alone. He came back to his own wife. “You're angry,” said Cú Chulainn. “I'm not angry,” said Ethne, “because it's from me they were given out.” … “Don't be angry, then,” said Cú Chulainn. “If birds come to the plain of Muirthemne or the Boyne, the two loveliest of them shall be yours.”",
          n: "He makes a promise to his wife: the two loveliest birds. The next two birds are the otherworld." },
        { w: "§7. Ni bo chian iarom, co n-accatar da en forsind loch, ocus rond dergóir etorro. Cansit céol m-bec. Torchair cotlud forsin slóg. Atraig Cuculaind a n-docom. «Dia coistithe frim», or Lóeg ocus ol Ethne, «ni rigtha chucu, ar itá nach cumachta for a cúl na n-én sa. Atethatar éoin dam-sa chena.»",
          e: "It was not long before they saw two birds on the lake, with a chain of red gold between them. They sang a soft music. Sleep fell on the host. Cú Chulainn got up to go after them. “If you'd listen to me,” said Láeg, and Ethne said it too, “you wouldn't go near them, for there is some power behind those birds. Other birds can be caught for me.”",
          n: "Two birds linked by a red-gold chain, singing the host to sleep. <strong>Compare Aislinge Óenguso</strong>, where Cáer and her companions become swans linked by silver chains every other Samhain. The chained birds are otherworld women. Láeg and Ethne both warn him. <strong>Compare Muirchertach</strong>, where every warning is understood and ignored." },
        { w: "«In dóig bat dom éligud-sa ón?» ol Cuculainn. «Gaibthi cloich isin tailm, a Lóig!» Geibthi Lóeg iarom cloich ocus dobeir isin tailm. Dos leci Cuculainn cloich foraib. Focairt imroll. «Fe amae!» ol se. Gaibid cloich n-aile. Dos leic doib, ocus luid seocu. «Am trúag-sa tra», ol se, «ó gabus-sa gaisced, ni ro la imroll mo urchur cussin diu.» Fochairt a chróisig forro, co lluid tré scíath n-ete indala héoin la sodain. Lotar fo lind.",
          e: "“Do you think they'll get away from me?” said Cú Chulainn. “Put a stone in the sling, Láeg!” Láeg took a stone and put it in the sling. Cú Chulainn let fly at them, and missed. “Alas!” he said. He took another stone and threw it, and it went past them. “Then I'm a wretch,” he said. “Since the day I took up arms, my throw has never missed until today.” He threw his spear at them, and it went through the wing-feathers of one of the birds. They went under the water.",
          n: "<em>ó gabus-sa gaisced, ni ro la imroll mo urchur cussin diu</em>: “since I took up arms my cast has never missed until today”. The first failure of his life, on the Samhain plain." },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "III. The two women at the pillar-stone",
      segments: [
        { w: "§8. Dothaet Cuculainn iarsin, co tard a druim frisin liic, ocus ba olc a menma leis, ocus dofuit cotlud fair. Co n-accai in da mnái cucai, indala n-ai brat úaine impe, alaili brat corcra cóicdíabail im sude.",
          e: "Then Cú Chulainn went and set his back against the stone, and he was in a bad mood, and sleep fell on him. And he saw two women coming towards him, one with a green cloak about her, the other with a five-fold crimson cloak.",
          n: "The pillar-stone (<em>corthe</em>, §§12–13) is the threshold here: he will come back to the same stone a year later to meet the otherworld again. Green and crimson are the two colours of the otherworld women in Irish. <strong>Compare Muirchertach</strong>: Sín's green mantle." },
        { w: "Dolluid in ben cosin brot úane chucai ocus tibid gen fris, ocus dobert beim dind echfleisc dó. Dothaet alaili cucai dano ocus tibid fris ocus nod slaid fón alt cétna, ocus batár fri ciana móir oca sin, .i. cechtar de immasech cucai beus dia bualad, co m-bo marb acht bec. Lotir úad iarom.",
          e: "The woman in the green cloak came up to him, and smiled at him, and struck him a blow with a horsewhip. The other came to him too, and smiled at him, and beat him the same way. And they were at it a long time, each of them in turn coming to beat him again, until he was all but dead. Then they went away from him.",
          n: "<em>tibid gen fris</em>, “she smiles at him”, and then she whips him. The image at the heart of the tale: two smiling women taking turns to beat the greatest warrior in Ireland nearly to death with horsewhips, while he sleeps. It is both desire and punishment, and the text does not say which." },
        { w: "§9. Arigsitar Ulaid uli ani sin ocus asbertatar, ara n-duscide. «Acc», ol Fergus, «nachin glúasid, res atchi.» Atracht iarom trena chotlud. «Cid dot rónad?» ol Ulaid fris. Ni ro fet iarom a n-acallaim. «Nom berar», for se, «dom sergligu, .i. don Teti Bricc, na bá do Dun Imrith no do Dún Delca.» … Berair ass iarom, co m-bói co cend m-bliadna isin magin sin cen labrad fri nech etir.",
          e: "All the Ulstermen noticed this, and said he should be woken. “No,” said Fergus, “don't move him; he is seeing something.” Then he woke from his sleep. “What was done to you?” the Ulstermen asked him. He could not speak to them. “Let me be carried to my sickbed,” he said, “to the Téte Brecc — not to Dún Imrith or to Dún Delga.” … So he was carried off, and he was there until the end of a year without speaking to anyone at all.",
          n: "<em>res atchí</em>, “he is seeing a vision”. Fergus knows not to wake a man who is in the otherworld. <em>serglige</em>, “wasting-bed”: the tale's title. A year without speech. <strong>Compare Muirchertach</strong>, whose wasting comes from eating the otherworld's food. Here it comes from being touched by it." },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "IV. A year in bed; a stranger at Samhain",
      segments: [
        { w: "§10. Lathi n-and resin t-samfuin aile cind bliadna, a m-bátar Ulaid imbi isin taig, .i. Fergus etir ocus fraigid, Conall Cernach etir ocus crand, Lugaid Reóderg etir ocus adart, Ethne Inguba fria chossa, a m-batar iarom fón samail sin, tánic fer chucu isa tech ocus dessid forsind airiniuch na imdai i m-bói Cuculainn.",
          e: "One day before the next Samhain, at the end of the year, the Ulstermen were round him in the house: Fergus between him and the wall, Conall Cernach between him and the bed-post, Lugaid Réoderg between him and the pillow, Ethne Inguba at his feet. While they were placed like that, a man came into the house and sat down on the front of the bed where Cú Chulainn lay.",
          n: "Exactly a year later, on the eve of the next Samhain. The household is arranged round the bed like guards at the four sides, and a stranger walks straight through them and sits on the bed. The otherworld comes in at the festival, past the household, to the bed: the innermost ring." },
        { w: "«Cid dot tucai and sin?» ol Conall Cernach. «Ninsa», ol se. «Mád ina sláinti ind fir fil sund, ro bad chomairche ar Ultaib ulib; mád i lobrai ocus i n-ingás dano ata, is móo de as comairche airthiu. Ni agur nech iarom, uair is dia acallaim dodeochad.» «Tathut fáelte, ni aigther ni!» ol Ulaid.",
          e: "“What brings you here?” said Conall Cernach. “Easy to say,” he said. “If the man here were in health, he would be protection enough against all Ulster. If he is sick and weak, he is a greater protection still. So I fear no one, for it is to speak with him that I have come.” “Welcome to you; fear nothing!” said the Ulstermen." },
        { w: "§11. Atraig iarsin inna sesam ocus gabais doib inna randa sa sis: «A Cuculaind, fot galar ni bo sirsan in t-anad, / not ícfitís, díamtis lat, ingena Aeda Abrát. / Asbert Liban i maig Cruaich bis for deis Labrada Luaith, / ro bad cridiscel la Faind coibligi fri Coinculaind. // Ro bad inmain lá, mád fír ricfed Cuchulaind mo thír, / rambiad arcat ocus ór, rombiad mór fina do ól. / Diammad chara dam cose Cuchulaind mac Soalte, / inatconnarc ina súan bes atcoad cen a slúag. // I m-Maig Murthemni sút teis aidchi samna, nib amles, / dot ficfe uaim-se Liban, a Chuchulaind, cot galar.»",
          e: "Then he stood up and sang them these verses: “Cú Chulainn, in your sickness, waiting here will do no good. / The daughters of Áed Abrát would heal you, if they were with you. / Lí Ban, who sits at the right hand of Labraid the Swift, said on the plain of Crúach: / it would be the heart's desire of Fand to lie with Cú Chulainn. // ‘Dear to me the day, if it were true, that Cú Chulainn came to my land; / he would have silver and gold, he would have much wine to drink. / If he were my friend from now on, Cú Chulainn son of Súaltam, / what he saw in his sleep he might see, without his army.’ // On the plain of Muirthemne, there, on Samhain night — it will do no harm — / Lí Ban will come to you from me, Cú Chulainn, to your sickness.”",
          n: "The invitation, in verse, at Samhain: <em>teis aidchi samna</em>, “there, on Samhain night”. Windisch notes that O'Curry mistranslated <em>not ícfitís</em>, which means “they would heal you”. The healers and the wounders are the same two women." },
        { w: "§12. «Coich thussa?» ol iat. «Messi Óengus mac Aeda Abrát», ol se. Luid úadib iarom in fer, ocus ni fetatar, cia deochaid no can don luid. Atraig Cuchulaind ina sudi iarom ocus labrais iarsin. «Bá mithig ém», ol Ulaid, «ani sin: innis cid a n-dot rónad.» «Atconnarc ém», ol se, «aislingi immon samain in n-uraid.» Adfét dóib uli amal atchonnairc. «Cid dogentar di sudiu, a popa Choncobair?» ol Cuchulaind. «Dogéntar», ol Concobar, «ort, co ris in corthe cétna.»",
          e: "“Who are you?” they said. “I am Óengus son of Áed Abrát,” he said. Then the man went from them, and they did not know where he went or where he had come from. Cú Chulainn sat up and spoke. “It's high time,” said the Ulstermen. “Tell what was done to you.” “I saw a vision,” he said, “at Samhain last year.” He told them all he had seen. “What shall be done about it, father Conchobar?” said Cú Chulainn. “This shall be done,” said Conchobar. “Get up and go to the same stone.”",
          n: "He has his voice back, a year later, on the eve of Samhain. Conchobar's ruling is a threshold rule: go back to <em>the same stone</em>." },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "V. Lí Ban at the stone; Láeg goes to Mag Mell",
      segments: [
        { w: "§13. Luid Cuchulaind ass iarom, co ránic in corthe, co n-accai in mnái bruit úaine chucai. «Maith sin, a Chuchulaind», ol si. «Ni maith dún ém. Cid for techt-si chucund in n-uraid?» ol Cuchulaind. «Ni du for fogail ém», ol si, «dodeochammár-ni, acht is do chuinchid for caratraid.»",
          e: "So Cú Chulainn went to the stone, and saw the woman in the green cloak coming towards him. “That's well, Cú Chulainn,” she said. “It is not well for me,” said Cú Chulainn. “Why did you come to me last year?” “It wasn't to harm you that we came,” she said, “but to seek your friendship.”" },
        { w: "«Dodeochad-sa ém dot acallaim-siu», ol in ben, «o Faind ingin Aeda Abrát; ros léci Manandan mac Lir, ocus dorat seirc duit-siu iarom. Liban dano m'ainm-se fein. Timarnad duit iarom óm céliu, o Labraid Luathlam ar claideb: Dobéra deit in mnai ar debaid n-óenlai leis fri Senach Siaborthe ocus fri Echdáig n-Iúil ocus fri Eogan n-Inbir.»",
          e: "“I have come to speak with you,” said the woman, “from Fand daughter of Áed Abrát. Manannán son of Ler has left her, and she has given her love to you. Lí Ban is my own name. I have a message for you from my husband, Labraid Swift-Hand-on-Sword: he will give you the woman in return for one day's fighting on his side against Senach the Spectral, Eochaid Iúil and Eógan Inbir.”",
          n: "The bargain: a woman for a day's fighting. The enemies' names are spectral: <em>Senach Siaborthe</em>, “Senach the Phantom”." },
        { w: "«Nimtha maith ém», ol se, «do chath fri firu indiu.» «Bid gar úar ani sin», or Liban, «bia slán, ocus doformastar deit a n-dotesta dit nirt. Is denta dait ar Labraid ani sin, ar is e láech as dech di ócaib domain.» «Ci-si airm hita side?» for Cuchulaind. «Itá i m-Maig Meli», ol si. «Is ferr dam-sa techt leth n-aill», ol ind ingen. «Taet Lóeg lat», ol Cuchulaind, «d'fis in tíri asa tudchad.» «Taet iarom», ol Liban.",
          e: "“I'm in no state to fight men today,” he said. “That will soon pass,” said Lí Ban. “You will be healed, and what is lacking of your strength will be added to you. You must do this for Labraid, for he is the best warrior among the young men of the world.” “Where is he?” said Cú Chulainn. “He is in Mag Mell,” she said. “It would be better for me to go to the other side now,” said the woman. “Let Láeg go with you,” said Cú Chulainn, “to learn about the land you have come from.” “Let him come, then,” said Lí Ban.",
          n: "<strong>Mag Mell</strong>, the Plain of Delight, an otherworld across water. Cú Chulainn will not go himself on a woman's invitation. He sends his charioteer to look first: the first scout's report from the otherworld in the series." },
        { w: "§14. Lotar iarom, co rancatar co airm i m-bói Fand. Tic iarom Liban dia saigid Lóig ocus geibthi ar gúalaind. «Ni raga ass tra, a Lóig», ol Liban, «indiu i m-bethu, acht ma 'tit ainge ben.» «Ni bo ed as mó ro gnáthaigsem dún cus trath sa», for Lóeg, «bancomairchi.» «Appraind ocus bithappraind, nach hé Cuchulaind fil it richt indossa», or Liban. «Bád maith lim-sa dano, co m-bad hé no beth and», for Lóeg.",
          e: "So they went until they came to where Fand was. Lí Ban took hold of Láeg by the shoulder. “You won't come out of here alive today, Láeg,” said Lí Ban, “unless a woman protects you.” “A woman's protection isn't what we've been most used to until now,” said Láeg. “A pity, and a lasting pity, that it isn't Cú Chulainn who is in your shape now,” said Lí Ban. “I'd like it too, if it were he who was here,” said Láeg.",
          n: "A threat with a smile: <em>you will not leave here alive, unless a woman protects you</em>. Láeg is a stand-in, and both of them know it." },
        { w: "§15. Lotar ass iarom, co rancatar taebh na hinse, co n-accatar in lungine crédume forsind loch ar a cind. Tiagait iarom isin lunga ocus tíagait isin n-insi ocus lotar do dorus tige, co n-accatar in fer chucu. Is and asbert Liban fris: «Cate Labraid Luathlám ar claideb, as cend m-buden m-búada, / búaid úas ehret charpait glinni, dercas rinni ruada?» Frisgart dissi in fer iarsin: «Atá Labraid luithe cland, ni bá mall, bid imda, / tinol catha, cuirthir ár, dia bá lan Mag Fidga.»",
          e: "They went on until they came to the shore of the island, and saw a little bronze boat on the lake before them. They got into the boat and crossed to the island, and went to the door of a house, and saw a man coming towards them. Then Lí Ban said to him: “Where is Labraid Swift-Hand-on-Sword, head of the victorious bands, / victory above a strong chariot, who reddens red spear-points?” The man answered her: “Labraid is gathering his clans — he will not be slow, they will be many — / a mustering for battle; slaughter will be done, until Mag Fidga is full.”",
          n: "Across the water in a bronze boat to an island: the Irish otherworld as an island over a lake, as in the voyage tales." },
        { w: "§16. Tíagait iarom isa tech, co n-accatar tri cóecta imdad is tig, ocus tri cóicait ban indib. Fersait ule fáelti fri Lóeg. Is ed asbertatar uli fris: «Fochen duit, a Lóig, diag neich las tudchad, / ocus o tudchad, is dit dáig féisni.» … «Ata i n-airicul fo leith», ol Liban. Lotar iarom dia hacallaim ocus ferais side fáelte friu fon innas cetna.",
          e: "They went into the house, and saw thrice fifty couches in it, and thrice fifty women on them. They all made Láeg welcome. This is what they all said to him: “Welcome to you, Láeg, for the sake of the one with whom you came, / and for the sake of the one from whom you came, and for your own sake too.” … “She is in a chamber apart,” said Lí Ban. They went to speak with her, and she welcomed them in the same way." },
        { w: "§17. Fand dano ingen Aeda Abrát, .i. aed tene, is he tene na súla, in mac imlesen; fand iarom ainm na dére dothét tairis. Ar a glaini ro ainmniged disi sin ocus ar a cóimi, ar ni bói isin bith frisa samailte chena.",
          e: "Fand, then, daughter of Áed Abrát. <em>Áed</em> means fire, and that is the fire of the eye, the pupil; and <em>fand</em> is the name of the tear that runs over it. She was named that for her purity and her loveliness, for there was nothing else in the world she could be compared to.",
          n: "An etymology inside the story: Fand is <em>the tear on the eye</em>, daughter of the eye's fire. Her very name is a sorrow. <strong>Compare the Names instrument</strong>: Sín is Storm, the man at the ford is Cold-Wind, and Fand is a Tear." },
        { w: "In tan m-bátar and iarom, co cúalatar culgaire carpait Labrada dond insi. «Is olc menma Labrada indiu», ol Liban. «Tiagam dia acallaim.» Tiagait ass immach ocus ferais Liban fáelti fris: «Fochen Labraid Luath lam ar claideb, / comarbe buidne snede slegaige, / slaidid sciathu, scailid gáu, / créchtnaigid curpu, gonaid sóeru … fochen Labraid.» [§18] Nis frecart Labraid beus, ocus asbert ind ingen atheruch … Ni ro regart beus Labraid; canaid si láid n-aili afridissi.",
          e: "While they were there they heard the rattle of Labraid's chariot coming to the island. “Labraid is in a bad mood today,” said Lí Ban. “Let us go and speak to him.” They went out, and Lí Ban welcomed him: “Welcome, Labraid Swift-Hand-on-Sword, / heir of bands of small spears; / he smashes shields, he scatters spears, / he wounds bodies, he slays freemen … welcome, Labraid.” [§18] Labraid still did not answer her, and the woman spoke again… Still Labraid did not answer; she sang another lay to him again.",
          n: "Three greetings in <em>rosc</em>, the dense alliterating verse, each praising his violence. Translated tentatively here: Windisch shows O'Curry mistook the verbs for nouns. Labraid answers none of them, the silence of a king before a battle he may lose." },
        { w: "§19. «Ni maith a n-asberi, a ben», ol inti Labraid, conid and asbert: «Ni úall na úabur dam, a ben, … rechmi cath n-imrind n-imda n-imamnas, / imberta claideb n-derg ar dornaib desaib … ni úall, ni úabar dam, a ben.»",
          e: "“It's not good, what you're saying, woman,” said Labraid, and then he said: “It is not pride or arrogance in me, woman… a battle is coming, of many points, many and fierce, / a red sword will be plied in right hands… / It is not pride, it is not arrogance in me, woman.”",
          n: "He refuses the praise. The best warrior in the world is afraid of the coming battle. Tentative translation." },
        { w: "§20. «Bad maith lat do menma tra», ol in ben, Liban, fris. «Ata Lóeg ara Conculaind sund, ocus timarnad duit úad, dot icfa slóg úad.» Ferais Labraid fáelti fris iarom a n-asbert: «Fochen duit, a Láig, fo bith na mná las tánac ocus in cháich o tudchad. Do duit do tig, a Láig», or Labraid, «ocus ragaid Liban it diáid.» Tic Lóeg ass iarom co Emain ocus adfet a scéla do Choinculaind ocus do chach olchena. Atraig Cuchulaind iarsin na sudi ocus dobert láim dar a agid ocus acallais Lóeg co glé, ocus ba nertiti leis a menma na scéla adfiadar do in gilla.",
          e: "“Be glad, then,” said the woman, Lí Ban, to him. “Láeg, Cú Chulainn's charioteer, is here, and there is a message for you from him: a host will come to you from him.” Then Labraid welcomed him, saying: “Welcome to you, Láeg, for the sake of the woman you came with, and of everyone you came from. Go to your home, Láeg,” said Labraid, “and Lí Ban will follow you.” Láeg came back to Emain and told his news to Cú Chulainn and to everyone else. Cú Chulainn sat up and passed his hand over his face and spoke clearly to Láeg, and his spirits were strengthened by the news the boy told him.",
          n: "The scout's report heals. He sits up and <em>passes his hand over his face</em>: the gesture that marks the return of his senses, as it does again in §31." },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "VI. The bull-feast at Tara (an interpolation)",
      segments: [
        { w: "§21. Bai dano terchomrac oc cethri ollchoicedaib hErend ind inbaid sin, dús in faigbitis nech bad toga leo dia tibertis rigi n-Erend. Uair ba olc leo tulach airechais ocus tigernais hErend, .i. Temair, a bith cen rechtgi rig forri … Ar bátar fir hErend cen smacht rig forro fri re secht m-bliadan iar n-dith Conaire i m-Bruidin Dá Derga.",
          e: "At that time there was a gathering of four of the great provinces of Ireland, to see whether they could find someone they would choose to give the kingship of Ireland to. They thought it wrong that the hill of assembly and lordship of Ireland, Tara, should be without a king's rule… For the men of Ireland had been without a king's rule for seven years, since the death of Conaire in the Hostel of Dá Derga.",
          n: "Here Windisch marks an interpolation, set in “clearly in the wrong place”. It is a separate story that touches the main tale only because the chosen king will be found standing at Cú Chulainn's sickbed. It keeps its place in the manuscript and it keeps its place here." },
        { w: "§22–23. … Dognither iarom tarbfes leo and sin, co fiastais esti, cia dia tibertais rigi. Is amlaid dognithe in tarbfes sin, .i. tarb find do marbad ocus óen fer do caithim a sátha dia eóil ocus dia enbruithi, ocus cotlud do fón saith sin, ocus ór firindi do cantain do cethri druidib fair; ocus atchithe do i n-aislingi innas ind fir nó rígfaide and, asa deilb ocus asa tuarascbail ocus innas ind oibrid dognith.",
          e: "… So a bull-feast was held, so that they might learn from it to whom they should give the kingship. This is how the bull-feast was done: a white bull was killed, and one man ate his fill of its flesh and of its broth, and slept on that fill, and four druids chanted a spell of truth over him. And he would see in a dream the shape of the man who should be made king there: his form and his appearance, and the kind of work he was doing.",
          n: "The <em>tarbfeis</em>, the bull-sleep: a divination by sleeping on a sacrificed bull's meat while druids chant. It is one of the few descriptions in Irish of what looks like a pagan rite, and it is told as an aside in a love-story. The dream works: it shows a young man <em>with two red stripes about him, standing over the pillow of a sick man in Emain Macha</em>." },
        { w: "§24. Faiditir iarom techta frisin co Emain. … «Fil linni mac sóer soceneoil fon samail sin», ol Concobar, «.i. Lugaid Réoderg mac Na Tri Find Emna, dalta Conculaind, fil os adart na himda thall amne oc urgartigud a aite, .i. Conculaind, fil hi sirg.» Atraig Cuculaind andaide ocus gebid for tecosc a daltai.",
          e: "Messengers were sent to Emain about it. … “We have a noble, well-born youth of that likeness,” said Conchobar: “Lugaid Red-Stripes, son of the Three Finns of Emain, Cú Chulainn's foster-son, who stands over there at the pillow of the bed watching over his foster-father Cú Chulainn, who is wasting.” Then Cú Chulainn rose up and began to instruct his foster-son.",
          n: "The dream was right. The king of Ireland is found where the dream put him: at the head of the sickbed." },
        { w: "§25–26. Briatharthecosc Conculaind inso. «Nir bat taerrechtach debtha dene doérgairce. Nir bat áiscir dóichlech díummasach. … Ni fresnesea co labur. Ni aisneisea co glórach. Ní fuirse. Ní chuitbe. Ní faitchither senóri. … Bat umal múnta ó gáethaib. Bat cumnech coisc ót senaib. … Ni pat úarcraidech im chardiu. Bat gusmar im naimtiu. … Nir bat lesc, ar nar bat meirb.»",
          e: "This is the Word-Instruction of Cú Chulainn. “Do not be a quick and quarrelsome provoker of fights. Do not be wild, grudging or proud. … Do not tell things noisily. Do not talk loudly. Do not mock. Do not jeer. Do not scorn the old. … Be humble, taught by the wise. Be mindful of correction from your elders. … Do not be cold-hearted to your friends. Be forceful towards your enemies. … Do not be lazy, so that you will not be feeble.”",
          n: "A wisdom-text, a mirror for princes in the form of advice, set in the mouth of the man lying in a wasting-bed. Abridged here. Much of it is obscure, and O'Curry's 1858 rendering, which Windisch prints, is full of query-marks. This instrument-free piece has nothing to do with the otherworld except its setting: the sickbed is where Ireland's kingship is decided." },
        { w: "§27. Is and asbert Lugaid in so sis fri Coinculaind: «Ed as maith a m-bith uile, arin festar cach duine, / no co teseba ni de, firfaider mád urise.» Luid Lugaid iarsin frisna techtaib co Temraig, ocus gongarar garm rigi do, ocus fáis hi Temraig ind aidchi sin, ocus luid cach dia menat iarsin.",
          e: "Then Lugaid said this to Cú Chulainn: “That is good, in all the world, that everyone should know; / until nothing of it is lacking, it will be done, if it is possible.” Then Lugaid went with the messengers to Tara, and the king's name was proclaimed for him, and he slept in Tara that night, and everyone went home after that." },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "VII. Emer",
      segments: [
        { w: "§28. Imthusa immorro Conculaind iss ed adfiastar sund coleic: «Do duit uaim, a Láig», for Cuchulaind, «co airm hi ta Emer, ocus innis, condat mná síde rom thathigset ocus rom admilset, ocus apair fria is ferr a chách itossa, ocus tat dom indnaigid.»",
          e: "As for Cú Chulainn, this is what is told of him now. “Go from me, Láeg,” said Cú Chulainn, “to where Emer is, and tell her that it is women of the síd who came to me and destroyed me; and tell her I am better now, and that she should come to visit me.”",
          n: "From here the wife is <strong>Emer</strong>, not Ethne Inguba: the second version of the tale begins. <em>mná síde rom thathigset ocus rom admilset</em>, “women of the síd have haunted me and destroyed me”." },
        { w: "Is and asbert in gilla oc nertad Conculaind inso: «Mór espa do láech laigi fri súan serglige, / ar dot adbat genaiti, déssa a Tenmag Trogaigi, / condot rodbsat, condot chiachtsat, / condot ellat eter briga banespa. / Diuchtra a terbaig andregoin … / Dia focart lúth Labrada, a fir, rudi atrai co ro pat mór.»",
          e: "Then the boy said this, to rouse Cú Chulainn: “Great idleness for a warrior, to lie in the sleep of a wasting-bed, / for women have shown themselves to you, women from the Plain of Delight, / who have tamed you, who have bound you, / who have caught you in the snares of women's idleness. / Rise up from the sickness the women gave you… / Labraid's strength has called you, man: rise, so that you may be great!”",
          n: "A rousing <em>rosc</em>. The manuscript itself glosses the hard words: <em>genaiti</em> means women, <em>Tenmag Trogaigi</em> is Mag Mell, and the wounding is <em>a galar bansídi</em>, “the sickness of the síd-women”. Tentative." },
        { w: "§29. Téit in gilla iarsin co airm i m-bói Emer ocus adfet amal bói Cuchulaind. «Olc duit-siu, a gilli», for si, «ar is tu taithiges in síd cen feib ica do tigerna d'agbáil lat. Truag d'Ultaib», for si, «cen sirtin a márica.» … Cachain-si iarom láid la sodain:",
          e: "The boy went to where Emer was and told her how Cú Chulainn was. “Shame on you, boy,” she said, “for you go in and out of the síd and have found no cure for your lord there. Shame on the Ulstermen,” she said, “for not searching for his cure.” … Then she sang a lay:" },
        { w: "«… Truag d'Ultaib co lín garta eter aite is chomalta, / cen siriud in domain duind d'icc a carat Conculaind. // Mád Fergus no beth i ssúan, dán iccad aicned oendrúad, / ni biad mac Dechtere i fos, co fagbad drúi dia tomos. // … Uchan do galur nom geib ó Choin cherda Conchobair! / isseth rem chridi is rem cnes, dia tisad dim a leges. // … Mí is ráithe is bliadain cen chotlud fó chomríagail, / cen duini bad bind labra ni chúala, a mic Ríangabra.»",
          e: "“… A shame on the Ulstermen, with all their generosity, foster-father and foster-brother, / not to search the brown world to heal their friend Cú Chulainn. // If Fergus were in this sleep, and one druid's skill could heal him, / Dechtire's son would not rest until he found a druid to measure it. // … Alas for the sickness that takes me, from Conchobar's crafty Hound! / It is in my heart and in my skin — if only his cure would come from me. // … A month, a season and a year without sleep, keeping watch, / without hearing a sweet-voiced person, son of Ríangabair.”",
          n: "Emer's lament, abridged here (eight more quatrains list each Ulster hero who would have been searched for). A year without sleep: <em>bliadain cen chotlud</em>. The household that lies awake is the series' recurring image. <strong>Compare Þiðrandi</strong>, where the household lies awake pretending to sleep." },
        { w: "§30. Tanic Emer rempi co hEmain iarsin d'innaigid Conculaind ocus dessid issind imdái i m-bái Cuculaind, ocus ro bái cá rád: «Is mebul duit», or si, «laigi fri bangrád, uair dogenad galar duit sírligi.» Ocus ro chan láid: «Erig, a gerait Ulad, rodúsci súan slán subach … / déca a chairptiu cinnit glend, déca arretha fian fidchell … / Is meth, ni maith cotlud trom, is mertan ar n-écomlond … / Rodusig súan síd ar n-ól … ilar m-briathar m-bláith rot char: erig, a gerait Ulad!»",
          e: "Then Emer came to Emain to Cú Chulainn and sat down on the bed where he lay, and she said: “Shame on you, to lie down for a woman's love; for it would bring you long sickness.” And she sang a lay: “Rise, champion of Ulster, wake from a sound and happy sleep… / see their chariots crossing the glens, see the war-bands playing fidchell… / Heavy sleep is decay, it is no good; it is weakness against an unequal fight… / The sleep of the síd has taken you… many soft words have loved you: rise, champion of Ulster!”",
          n: "<em>Is mebul duit laigi fri bangrád</em>: “shame on you, lying down for a woman's love”. Emer names the sickness for what it is." },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "VIII. Into the otherworld; the battle",
      segments: [
        { w: "§31. Atracht iarom Cuchulaind iarsin ocus dorat láim dar a agid, ocus ro chuir a mertnigi ocus a tromdacht de, ocus atracht iarsin ocus tanic reme iarsin, co m-boi i n-airbi ro fir. Co n-acca chuci iarsin Liban, ocus ro ráid ind ingen friss ocus bái oc a thócuriud dond t-síd. «Ci-si airm hi tá Labraid?» ol Cuchulaind. «Ninsa», ol si:",
          e: "Then Cú Chulainn rose, and passed his hand over his face, and threw off his weakness and his heaviness, and rose and went on until he was at the place he knew. Then he saw Lí Ban coming towards him; the woman spoke to him and invited him to the síd. “Where is Labraid?” said Cú Chulainn. “Easy to say,” she said:" },
        { w: "«Ata Labraid for lind glan dia n-aithiget buidni ban, / ni ba scith let techt dia thuaid, mád ar fis Labrada Luaith. … / Corcair co n-áldi datha samail grúadi Labrada. … / Folt fair amal flesca óir, bolad fína lia anáil. … / Srian muinci dergóir fria graig, ocus noconed namma; / úatni airgit ocus glain, iss ed fil is tig hi ta.»",
          e: "“Labraid is on a clear lake, which bands of women visit; / you won't be tired going north to him, if it's to see Labraid the Swift. … / Crimson with beautiful colours, like Labraid's cheeks. … / Hair on him like rods of gold, the smell of wine on his breath. … / Bridles and collars of red gold on his horses, and nothing else; / pillars of silver and of crystal: that is what is in the house where he is.”",
          n: "Lí Ban's description of Labraid and his house, abridged (twelve quatrains). Gold, crystal and wine: the otherworld is wealth and the scent of wine." },
        { w: "§32. «No co rag-sa», ar Cúculaind, «ar cuiriud mná.» «Ticed iarom», ar ind ingen, «Lóeg and sút d'fis cech réta.» … Atralacht Lóeg iarom lasin n-ingin, ocus dochuatar do Maig Luada ocus don Biliu Búada ocus dar Oenach n-Emna ocus i n-Óenach Fidga, ocus is and side bái Aed Abrát cona ingenaib. Feraid Fand fáilte fri Lóeg. «Cid dia m-bái Cuchulaind cen tiachtain?» or si. «Nir bo áil leiss tiachtain ar banchuiriud.» … «Is uaim», ar si, «ocus ticed co lúath diar saigid, ar is indiu curthir in cath.»",
          e: "“I won't go,” said Cú Chulainn, “on a woman's invitation.” “Then let Láeg go there,” said the woman, “to learn everything.” … So Láeg set off with the woman, and they went to Mag Lúada and to the Bile Búada, the Tree of Victory, and over Óenach Emna into Óenach Fidga; and there Áed Abrát was, with his daughters. Fand welcomed Láeg. “Why didn't Cú Chulainn come?” she said. “He didn't want to come on a woman's invitation.” … “It is from me,” she said, “and let him come quickly, for the battle is today.”",
          n: "He refuses the threshold a second time on the same ground, <em>ar cuiriud mná</em>, “on a woman's invitation”. The road passes a sacred tree (<em>bile</em>). <strong>Compare Muirchertach</strong>, where Duaibsech dies against one." },
        { w: "§33. Luid Lóeg atheroch co airm i m-boi Cuchulaind … ocus ro chan laid: «Ránac-sa rém rebrad rán baile ingnad, ciar bo gnád, / co ránic in carn fichtib drong hi fúar Labraid lebarmong. … / Atát ar in dorus tiar in sinnait hi fuined grian, / graig n-gabor n-glas, brec a mong, is araile corcordond. / Atát ar in dorus sair tri bile do chorcor glain, / dia n-gair in énlaith búan bláith don macraid assin rígráith.»",
          e: "Láeg went back to where Cú Chulainn was … and sang a lay: “I came, in my pleasant sporting, to a wonderful place, though it was familiar, / until I reached the cairn of the twenty bands, where I found long-haired Labraid. … / At the western door, where the sun sets, / there is a herd of grey horses with dappled manes, and another, red-brown. / At the eastern door are three trees of crimson crystal, / from which a gentle, unceasing flock of birds calls to the children from the royal fort.”",
          n: "The great description of Mag Mell, in Láeg's mouth. In the otherworld the house has thresholds too, east and west." },
        { w: "«Ata crand i n-dorus liss, ni hétig cocetul friss, / crand airgit ris taitin grian, cosmail fri hór a roniam. / Atat and tri fichit crand, comraic nad chomraic a m-barr, / biatar tri cét do cach crund do mes ilarda imlum. / Ata tipra sin t-síd thréll cona tri cóectaib breclend … / Dabach and do mid medrach oc a dáil for in teglach, / maraid beós, is búan in bés, conid bithlán do bithgrés. / Ita ingen is tig thréll ro derscaig do mnaib Erend, / co fult buide thic immach, issi álaind illánach.»",
          e: "“There is a tree at the door of the court — no music matches it — / a tree of silver that the sun shines on, its splendour like gold. / There are three score trees there whose tops meet and do not meet; / three hundred are fed from each tree with abundant, ripe fruit. / There is a well in that noble síd, with thrice fifty speckled cloaks… / There is a vat of merry mead there, being served to the household; / it is there still, the custom lasts, so that it is always full. / There is a girl in that noble house who surpasses the women of Ireland, / with yellow hair coming down, lovely and gifted.”",
          n: "The vat that is always full. <strong>Compare Muirchertach</strong>, where Sín fills three vats from the Boyne and the king drowns in one. The otherworld's endless drink, seen from the other side." },
        { w: "«Atnibairt ind ingen thréll: ‘Coich in gilla nad aichnem? / masa thú, tair bic ille, gilla ind fir a Murthemne.’ / Dochúadusa co foill foill, rom gab ecla dom onóir; / … Da m-bad lim Eriu uile ocus rige Breg m-buide, / dobérainn — ni láthar lac — ar gnais in baile ránac.»",
          e: "“The girl said then: ‘Who is the boy we don't know? / If it's you, come here a little, boy of the man from Muirthemne.’ / I went slowly, slowly; I was afraid for my honour. / … If all Ireland were mine, and the kingship of fair Brega, / I would give it — no weak bargain — to live in the place I came to.”",
          n: "The scout's verdict: he would trade all Ireland for it. <strong>Compare Muirchertach</strong>, who would give all Ireland for <em>the loan of her for one night</em>. The same price, paid in opposite directions." },
        { w: "§34. «Is maith sin», ar Cúculaind. «Is maith», ar Lóeg, «ocus is coir dul dia riachtain, ocus is maith cach ni issin tír sin.» Ocus is and asbert Lóeg beós fris-seom ic innisin oibninsa in t-sída: «Atconnarc tír sorcha sóer inna ráiter gó ná cloen … / Atconnarc láechu colli co n-armaib ic imdibi, / atconnarc étach n-datha … / Atconnarc mná féta ic fleid, atconnarc ingenraid, / atconnarc gillu glána oc imtecht ind fid dromma. … / Atconnarc in cnoc ro búi álaind ben Eithne Ingubai, / acht in ben atberar sund beres na slúagu asa cund.»",
          e: "“That's good,” said Cú Chulainn. “It is good,” said Láeg, “and it's right to go there, and everything in that land is good.” And Láeg went on telling him the delights of the síd: “I saw a bright, noble land, where no lie or falsehood is told. … / I saw tall warriors with their weapons, fighting; / I saw coloured clothing… / I saw fine women feasting, I saw maidens, / I saw bright youths going over the wooded ridge. … / I saw the hill where lovely Ethne Inguba was — / but the woman spoken of here would draw away the hosts from her head.”",
          n: "<em>tír sorcha sóer inna ráiter gó</em>, “a bright land where no lie is told”. A land without lies, in a tale full of them. Láeg's last lines rank Fand above Ethne: the first version's wife." },
        { w: "§35. Luid Cuchulaind lee iarom is tír ocus bert a charpat les, co rancatar in n-insi. Feraid Labraid fáelti friú, ocus fersi in bantrocht uli, ocus ferais Fand dano fáelti sainredaig fri Coinculaind. «Cid dogentar sund hi fecht sa?» ol Cuchulaind. «Ninsa», or Labraid, «iss ed dogenam, regmai, co rolam cor imón slúag.» … ocus bá dírim leó in slúag. «Eirg ass hi fecht sa», ol Cuchulaind fri Labraid. Luid Labraid ass iarom ocus anais Cuchulaind ocon t-slóg. Fanócrat in da fiach druidechta. … «Is doig», ol in slúag, «in riastartha a hErind, iss ed terchanait ind fiaich.»",
          e: "So Cú Chulainn went with her to that land, taking his chariot, and they came to the island. Labraid welcomed them, and all the women did, and Fand gave Cú Chulainn a special welcome. “What's to be done here now?” said Cú Chulainn. “Easy,” said Labraid. “We'll go and make a circuit round the host.” … And the host seemed countless to them. “Go away for now,” said Cú Chulainn to Labraid. Labraid went, and Cú Chulainn stayed facing the host. The two druidic ravens announced him. … “It must be,” said the host, “the Distorted One from Ireland that the ravens are foretelling.”",
          n: "He crosses at last, by chariot, onto the island. Two ravens announce him: <em>in ríastartha a hÉrinn</em>, “the Distorted One out of Ireland”, his battle-frenzy, known even in the otherworld." },
        { w: "§36. Dos sennat in t-slúaig iarom, conná fúair ined leó is tír. Dothéit Eochaid Iúil iarom do inlut a lam don tiprait matain moch. Atconnairc Cuchulaind iarom a gualaind tresin cochull. Doléci gai do, con luid trit. Ro marb triar for trichait dib a oenur. Tofobairt iarsin Senach Siabortha, ocus ferait mor gleó, ocus marbthus Cuchulaind iarom. Tic Labraid iarom ocus mebais riam forsna slógu.",
          e: "Then the hosts chased the ravens off, so that they found no place in the land. Early in the morning Eochaid Iúil went to wash his hands at the well. Cú Chulainn saw his shoulder through the cloak, and threw a spear at him that went through him. He killed thirty-three of them on his own. Then Senach the Spectral attacked him, and they fought a great fight, and Cú Chulainn killed him. Then Labraid came, and the hosts broke before him.",
          n: "A man washing his hands at a well at dawn, and a spear through his shoulder. The otherworld's battle is over in a paragraph." },
        { w: "Ro gáid Labraid do anad dind imguin. «Atagamar tra», for Lóeg, «in fer d'imbert a ferci fornd, úair nach lór leis di cath fúair. Tiagar», for Lóeg, «ocus inliter teora dabcha úarusci do dibdud a brotha. In cétna dabach i tét, fichid tairse; in dabach tanaise, nis fodaim nech ar a tes; in tres dabach, is comse a tes.»",
          e: "Labraid begged him to stop the slaughter. “Now we're afraid,” said Láeg, “that the man will turn his fury on us, since he hasn't had enough fighting. Go,” said Láeg, “and get three vats of cold water ready to cool his heat. The first vat he goes into boils over; the second, no one can bear for the heat; the third is just warm.”",
          n: "The three vats that cool his battle-heat, as in the Táin. <strong>Compare Muirchertach</strong>: there a vat of wine, here three vats of water. Vats appear in two tales, once to save the hero and once to drown him." },
        { w: "§37. In tan atconcatar na mná Coinculaind, is and cachain Fand in so: «Segda cairptech docing rót, cesu amulach is óc, / álaind lúadam luades blai fescur iar n-óenuch Fidgai. … / Fil secht suilse ar a rusc … abratchair duba daile. / … Claideb russi roindes crú cona imdurnd airgdidu … / Cuchulaind dothaet ille in t-ócláech a Murtemne, / is iat dorat sund hi fat ingena Aeda Abrát. / Bróenán fola fota fland la toeb crand comarda de, / úallach úabrech árd la gol, mairg fri siabra sé!»",
          e: "When the women saw Cú Chulainn, Fand sang this: “A stately chariot-warrior comes along the road, though he is beardless and young, / a beautiful drive he makes, in the evening, after the assembly of Fidga. … / There are seven lights in his eye… black, lovely eyebrows. / … A red sword that sheds blood, with a silver hilt… / Cú Chulainn comes here, the young warrior from Muirthemne; / it is they who brought him here, so far — the daughters of Áed Abrát. / A long red stream of blood beside a tree of equal height; / proud, arrogant, high in weeping — woe to whoever is a phantom against him!”",
          n: "Fand's praise, abridged. The last line, <em>mairg fri síabra sé</em>, “woe to a phantom who is against him”, is spoken by one of the síabra about a mortal." },
        { w: "§38. Ferais Liban fáelti fris iar tain: «Fochen Cuchulaind, torc torachtaide, / mál mór Maigi Murthemni … » «Cesc, cid doronais, a Chuchulaind?» or Liban fris. Is and asbert Cuchulaind: «Tarlucus urchur dom sleig i n-dúnad Eogain Inbir, / no con fetur — sochla sét — in búaid dorignius no in bét. … / Ro chuala cneit Echach Iúil, i socraidi labrait beoil, / mád fír co fír, bes nip cath, in t-urcur ma tarlacad.»",
          e: "Then Lí Ban welcomed him: “Welcome, Cú Chulainn, boar of the attack, / great prince of the plain of Muirthemne… ” “Tell us what you have done, Cú Chulainn,” said Lí Ban to him. Then Cú Chulainn said: “I made a cast of my spear into the camp of Eógan Inbir; / I do not know — a famous path — whether what I did was a victory or a crime. … / I heard the groan of Eochaid Iúil; friendly lips spoke; / if truth is truth, it may not have been a battle, the cast that was made.”",
          n: "<em>no con fetur in búaid dorignius no in bét</em>: “I do not know whether what I did was a victory or a crime”. The hero is unsure whether a killing in the otherworld counts. A spear thrown at a man washing his hands may not have been a battle." },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "IX. The tryst at the strand",
      segments: [
        { w: "§39. Fóid Cúculaind iarsin lasin n-ingin ocus anais mís ina farrad, ocus celebraid hi cind mís di, ocus atbert si frissium: «In baile», ar si, «atbéra-su frim-sa dul it chomdáil, ragat-sa.» Ocus is and dorónsat comdáil ic Ibur Cind Trachta.",
          e: "Then Cú Chulainn slept with the woman, and stayed a month with her. At the end of the month he took his leave of her, and she said to him: “Wherever you tell me to go to meet you, I will go.” And they made a tryst at Ibar Cind Trachta, the Yew-Tree at the Head of the Strand.",
          n: "A month in the otherworld, then home. Unlike Nera's or Muirchertach's, his return is unremarked. The tryst is set on the shore, a threshold between land and sea (and the sea is where her husband rules)." },
        { w: "Ro innis do Emir ani sin. Dorónta scena acci-side do marbad na ingine. Tánic ocus cóeca ingen lee connici in comdáil. Is and ro bói Cuchulaind ocus Lóeg oc immirt fidchilli, ocus ni ro airigset na mná chucu. Is and ro rathaig Fand ocus asbert fri Lóeg: «Feg-su, a Láig, ani atchiu-sa?» «Cid in sin?» ar Lóeg.",
          e: "Emer was told of it. She had knives sharpened to kill the woman. She came to the tryst with fifty women. Cú Chulainn and Láeg were there playing fidchell, and did not notice the women coming. Then Fand noticed, and said to Láeg: “Look, Láeg, at what I see.” “What is it?” said Láeg.",
          n: "<em>Óenét Emire</em>, the only jealousy of Emer, in the tale's second title. Fifty women with sharpened knives coming over the strand while the men play a board-game. <strong>Compare Nera</strong>, where Ailill and Fergus are at fidchell when the bull-calf bellows. In both, the game is interrupted by the thing the tale is about." },
        { w: "§40. Dercais Lóeg, ocus is and ro radi ind ingen in so, .i. Fand: [R.] «Feg, a Lóig, dar th'eis! / oc coistecht frit filet mná córi ciallmathi / co scenaib glasgéraib ina n-deslámaib … / glé ro sói gné Emer ingen Forgaill.» [R.] «Ní tágara», ar Cuchulaind, «ocus ni con tora eter. Tair-siu isin creit cumachta lasin suidi n-grianda … ar cia nos bagea ingen Forcaill … bés ni lim lamathair.»",
          e: "Láeg looked, and then the woman, Fand, said this: “Look behind you, Láeg! / Listening to you are proper, sensible women, / with sharp grey knives in their right hands… / Clearly, Emer daughter of Forgall has changed her look.” “Don't be afraid,” said Cú Chulainn, “she won't reach you at all. Come up into the powerful chariot, onto the sunny seat… for though Forgall's daughter threatens… perhaps she will not dare, while I am here.”",
          n: "Rosc again, translated tentatively." },
        { w: "§41. Asbert beós Cuchulaind: «Not sechnaim-sea, a ben, amal sechnas cách a charait; / ni rubim-sea do gáe cruaid crithlámach, / nách do scian timthanaidi, nách t'ferg threith timaircthech, / ar is mórdolig mo nert do scor ó nirt mná.» «Cesc tra», ar Emer, «cid fod ruair lat-su, a Chuchulaind, mo dímiad-sa fiad mnáib ilib in chóicid ocus fiad mnáib ilib na hErend … ar is fót chlith tánac-sa … bés ni pád rith lat-su mo lécud-sa, a gillai, cia no triallta.»",
          e: "Cú Chulainn said further: “I avoid you, woman, as everyone avoids a friend. / I will not strike your hard, trembling spear, / nor your thin knife, nor your weak, restrained anger; / for it is very hard for my strength to be overcome by a woman's.” “Tell me, then,” said Emer, “what made you shame me before the many women of the province and the many women of Ireland… for I came under your protection… Perhaps it will not be easy for you to leave me, boy, even if you try.”" },
        { w: "§42. «Cesc tra, a Emer», ar Cuchulaind, «cid ar na leicfidea dam-sa mo denus i n-dáil mná? ar chétus in ben-sa, issi in glan genmnaid gelgasta dingbála do rig … ind ingen sin do thonnaib dar lerab lánmóraib, co n-deilb ocus écosc ocus sóerchenel, co n-druni ocus lámda ocus lámthorud, co ceill ocus cond ocus cobsaidecht, co n-immad ech ocus bóthánte … A Emer», ar se, «ni fuigeba-su curaid cáin crechtach cathbúadach bá dam fiu-sa.»",
          e: "“Tell me, Emer,” said Cú Chulainn, “why won't you let me be with a woman for a while? For in the first place, this woman is pure, chaste, bright and clever, worthy of a king, the girl from over the waves of the full great seas, with beauty and looks and noble birth, with embroidery and handiwork, with sense and wisdom and steadiness, with plenty of horses and herds of cattle… Emer,” he said, “you will never find a fine, scarred, battle-winning champion who would be worth as much as me.”" },
        { w: "§43. «Bes», ar Emer, «no co n-err in ben dia lenai. Acht chena is álaind cech n-derg, is gel cach nua, is cáin cech ard, is serb cach gnáth, cáid cech n-écmais, is faill cech n-aichnid, co festar cach n-eólas. A gillai», ar si, «ro bámar-ni fecht co cátaid acut, ocus no bemmis dorisi, dia m-bad áil duit-siu.» Ocus ro bo dograch furri. «Dar mo brethir tra», ar se, «is at áil-siu dam-sa, ocus bid at áil, hi céin bat béo.»",
          e: "“Perhaps,” said Emer, “the woman you follow is no better. But then: everything red is beautiful, everything new is bright, everything high is lovely; everything familiar is bitter, everything absent is perfect, everything known is neglected, until all is known. Boy,” she said, “we were once in honour with you, and we would be again, if you wished it.” And grief came over her. “By my word,” he said, “you are dear to me, and you will be dear to me as long as you live.”",
          n: "Emer's great speech, the wisest passage in the series: <em>is serb cach gnáth, cáid cech n-écmais</em>, “everything familiar is bitter, everything absent is holy”. It is a whole theory of the otherworld's attraction in eight phrases, spoken by the wife at the edge of the sea." },
        { w: "§44. «Mo lécud-sa dino!» ol Fand. «Is córu mo lécud-sa», ar Emer. «Na thó», or Fand, «messi léicfidir and, ocus is mé ro báeglaiged o chéin.» Ocus forópair oc dogru ocus oc domenmain móir, ar bá nár lée a lécud ocus dul dia tig a chétóir, ocus ro buadir in rograd hi dorat do Coinculaind, ocus is amlaid ro bói oc dogru ocus doroni in laid sea:",
          e: "“Then let me be left!” said Fand. “It's more right that I should be left,” said Emer. “No,” said Fand, “it's I who will be left, and I who was in danger from the start.” And grief and great sadness came over her, for it was shameful to her to be left and to go straight home; and the great love she had given Cú Chulainn troubled her. And grieving, she made this lay:" },
        { w: "«Messe ragas for astur, ce dech lim ar mór gestul, / ce tha nech lin ablad, ro bad ferr lim tairisem. / … A Emer, is lat in fer, ocus romela, a deig ben; / ani ná roich lám cid acht, is écen dam a dútracht. / … Mairg dobeir seirc do duni mene thartar dia airi; / is ferr do neoch a chor ass, mene chartar mar charas.»",
          e: "“I am the one who will go on the journey, though I'd rather keep my great honour; / though there may be someone with more renown, I would rather have stayed. / … Emer, the man is yours, and may you enjoy him, good woman; / what my hand cannot reach, still I must wish it well. / … Woe to anyone who gives love to a person who will not notice it; / better to put it away, unless one is loved as one loves.”",
          n: "Fand's lament, abridged. <em>Mairg dobeir seirc do duni…</em>, “woe to one who gives love to someone who does not return it”: the otherworld woman as the abandoned one. <strong>Compare Muirchertach</strong>, where Sín dies of grief for the man she killed. Twice in the series the otherworld woman is left grieving." },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "X. Manannán's cloak",
      segments: [
        { w: "§45. Iarsin ro foillsiged do Manandan ani sin, .i. Fand ingen Aeda Abrát do bith i n-ecomlond ic mnáib Ulad ocus a bith co a lécud do Choinculaind. Tanic iarom Manannán anair do saigid na hingini, ocus ro bói ina fíadnaise, ocus ní ro rathaig nech díb aní sin acht Fand a hoenur. Ocus is and sin ro gab etere moir ocus drochmenmain in n-ingin oc fegad Manandán, ocus dorigni laid:",
          e: "Then Manannán learned of it: that Fand daughter of Áed Abrát was in an unequal fight with the women of Ulster, and that Cú Chulainn was about to leave her. So Manannán came from the east to the woman, and was there in front of her, and none of them noticed it except Fand alone. And then great anxiety and sadness took hold of the girl as she looked at Manannán, and she made a lay:",
          n: "<em>ní ro rathaig nech díb aní sin acht Fand a hóenur</em>, “none of them noticed except Fand alone”. The sea-god is present and invisible to everyone but his wife." },
        { w: "«Fegaid mac láechraidi Lir do maigib Eógain Inbir, / Manannán úas domun dind, ro bói tan rop inmain lim. / … La ro bá-sa ocus mac Lir hi n-grianan Dúni Inbir, / ro po dóig lind cen anad no co biad ar n-imscarad. / … Atchíu dar in muir ille — nin acend nach meraige — / marcach in mara mongaig, ni lenand do sithlongaib. / … Celebrad dit, a Chú cháin, a-so sind uait co sochraid … / Erge seó mithig dam-sa, ata nech risnid andsa … / Ragat rim chéli fodéin, dáig no co dingnea m'amréir; / nár apraid is céim i cleith, mád álic duib-si, fegaid!»",
          e: "“Look at the son of the warrior-host of Ler, from the plains of Eógan Inbir: / Manannán, over the bright world; there was a time when he was dear to me. / … One day, the son of Ler and I were in the sunny bower of Dún Inbir, / and we thought, without a doubt, that we would never part. / … I see coming over the sea — no one sees him who is not mad — / the horseman of the maned sea; he does not follow in ships. / … Farewell to you, fair Hound; we go from you gracefully… / It is time for me to rise; there is one for whom it is not hard. / I will go to my own husband, for he will not do my displeasure. / Don't say it is a secret going: look, if you like!”",
          n: "Fand's farewell. <em>marcach in mara mongaig</em>, “the rider of the maned sea”, on horseback over the waves, not in a ship. <em>nin accend nach meraige</em>, “no one sees him who is not mad”. It is visible only to the one who belongs to the otherworld, or to the mad." },
        { w: "§46. Atracht ind ingen iarsin i n-diáid Manannáin, ocus ro fer Manannán fáelti fria, ocus asbert: «Maith, a ingen», ar se, «in ocurnaidi Conculaind bia fodecht sa, no in lim-sa doraga?» «Dar ar m-brethir ém», ol si, «fil uaib nech bad ferr lim a chéli do lenmain. Acht», ar si, «is let-su ragat-sa, ocus ni irnaidiub Coinculaind, ar rom thréc; ocus araill and dano, a degduini, ni fil rigain catamail acot-su, ata immorro la Coinculaind.»",
          e: "Then the girl rose and went after Manannán, and Manannán welcomed her and said: “Well, girl, will you wait for Cú Chulainn now, or will you come with me?” “By our word,” she said, “there is one of you I would rather have as husband. But,” she said, “I will go with you, and I will not wait for Cú Chulainn, for he has abandoned me. And there is another thing, good man: you have no worthy queen with you, and Cú Chulainn has one.”",
          n: "She chooses with open eyes. She says plainly that she would rather have the other, and goes with her husband anyway, because the mortal has a wife and the god has none." },
        { w: "§47. O'tconnairc immorro Cuchulaind in n-ingin ic dula úad co Manannán, ro ráid fri Lóeg: «Crét sút?» ar sé. «Ninsa», ar Lóeg, «Fand ic dul la Manannán mac Lir, úair nír bo álic duit-siu hi.» Is and sin tra ro ling Cuchulaind tri ardléimenn ocus tri desléimenn Lúachra, corra bi fri ré fota cen dig cen biad sechnón na slébte, ocus is and no chotlad cech n-aidchi for Sligi Midlúachra.",
          e: "When Cú Chulainn saw the girl going away from him with Manannán, he said to Láeg: “What is that?” “Easy to say,” said Láeg. “Fand going away with Manannán son of Ler, because she was not good enough for you.” Then Cú Chulainn made three high leaps and three southward leaps of Lúachair, and he was a long time without drink or food, out on the mountains, and every night he slept on the Road of Midlúachair.",
          n: "Madness. He leaps, starves, and sleeps on a road, like the <em>geilt</em>, the wild madman of Irish tradition (Suibne in <em>Buile Shuibne</em>). The wasting comes back as madness when the otherworld leaves him." },
        { w: "§48. Dochoid tra Emer do saigid Conchobair co hEmain ocus ro innis do Cuchulaind amal ro bói. Ro fáid Conchobor filedu ocus áes dána ocus druidi Ulad dia saigid, co fastaitis ocus co tuctais co hEmain leó hé. Ro triall som dano in n-áes n-dána do marbad. Ro chansat side brechta druidechta ina agid, co ro gabait a chossa ocus a láma, conos tanic trell dia ceill.",
          e: "Then Emer went to Conchobar at Emain and told him how Cú Chulainn was. Conchobar sent the poets and the people of art and the druids of Ulster to him, to hold him and bring him back to Emain with them. He tried to kill the people of art. They sang druid-spells against him, so that his feet and his hands were held fast, until he came a little to his senses." },
        { w: "Ro bói seom dano oc cuingid digi chucu iar sin. Tucsat na druid dig n-dermait do. Amal atib in dig, nir bo chumain laiss Fand ocus cech ni doroni. Tucait dano deoga dermait a hét do Emir, ar nir bo ferr ro bói. Ro chroth dano Manannán a brat eter Coinculaind ocus Faind, conna ro chomraictis dogrés.",
          e: "Then he asked them for a drink. The druids gave him a drink of forgetting. As soon as he drank it he did not remember Fand, or anything he had done. They gave a drink of forgetting for her jealousy to Emer too, for she was no better off. And Manannán shook his cloak between Cú Chulainn and Fand, so that they would never meet again.",
          n: "<em>dig n-dermait</em>, the drink of forgetting. The cure for the otherworld is to forget it, and it is given to both of them. <strong>Compare Muirchertach</strong>, where the drinks of the otherworld cause the forgetting. Here the drinks of this world undo it. Manannán's cloak shaken between two lovers is the tale's last image: a threshold drawn by the sea-god himself." },
        { w: "§49. Conid taibsiu aidmillti do Choinchulaind la háes síde sin, ar ba mór in cumachta demnach ria cretim, ocus ba he a méit, co cathaigtis co corptha na demna frisna dóinib ocus co taisfentais áibniusa ocus díamairi dóib. Amal no betis comarthanach, is amlaid no creteá doib. Conid frisna taidbsib sin atberat na baneolaig side ocus áes sído.",
          e: "So that was a vision of destruction shown to Cú Chulainn by the people of the síd. For the demonic power was great before the Faith, and it was so great that the demons used to fight bodily against people, and show them delights and secrets, as if they were real; and so people believed in them. And it is these apparitions that the ignorant call síde and áes síde.",
          n: "The scribe's verdict, and the plainest case of the Two-readings instrument in the series. The story has just spent forty pages on a woman named for a tear, a sea-god on horseback, and a land where no lie is told. The last paragraph calls it all <em>taibsiu aidmillti</em>, a vision of destruction, worked by demons. <em>atberat na banéolaig</em>, “the ignorant say” (literally “the unlearned”), <em>side ocus áes sído</em>. <strong>Compare Þiðranda þáttr</strong>, whose compiler turns the dísir into the Devil, and <strong>Echtra Nerai</strong>, whose scribe writes <em>demnai</em>. Three scribes, the same move." },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
